.TH AVROSDB 1 "2026-05-17" "avrOSdb 0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
avrOSdb \- UPDI-to-GDB debug stub with avrOS FSM awareness
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B avrOSdb
.RB [ \-\-port
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-\-baud
.IR baud ]
.RB [ \-\-erase ]
.RB [ \-\-load ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-verify ]
.RB [ \-\-allow\-lock\-updi ]
.RB [ \-\-force\-device
.IR family ]
.I serial-device
.I elf-file
.br
.B avrOSdb
.B \-\-prog
.RB [ \-\-baud
.IR baud ]
.RB [ \-\-erase ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-verify ]
.RB [ \-\-allow\-lock\-updi ]
.RB [ \-\-force\-device
.IR family ]
.I serial-device
.I elf-file
.br
.B avrOSdb
.B \-\-device
.RB [ \-\-baud
.IR baud ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-autobaud ]
.RB [ \-\-force\-device
.IR family ]
.I serial-device
.RI [ elf-file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B avrOSdb
is a host-side GDB Remote Serial Protocol (RSP) server that bridges
.BR avr-gdb (1)
to AVR DA/DB targets over the UPDI single-wire debug interface.
It exposes each cooperative FSM task registered with the avrOS
runtime as a GDB virtual thread, so standard
.B info threads
and per-thread breakpoints work against an RTOS-aware view of the
target.
.PP
The server reads symbols and avrOS sentinel-table addresses from
.IR elf-file ,
opens
.I serial-device
as the UPDI UART, and listens on a TCP port for a single
.BR avr-gdb (1)
client. All UPDI memory access is non-intrusive when the CPU is
halted by GDB; no heap allocation occurs on the hot path.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-port " port"
TCP port to listen on for the
.BR avr-gdb (1)
client. Must satisfy
.RI 1 " " \(<= " " port " " \(<= " " 65535 .
Defaults to
.BR 1234 .
.TP
.BI \-\-baud " baud"
UART baud rate to the UPDI adapter. Must be a positive integer
accepted by the host
.BR termios (3)
layer. Defaults to
.BR 115200 .
.TP
.B \-\-load
Before entering the event loop, program every
.B PT_LOAD
segment of
.I elf-file
to the matching AVR-Dx NVM kind via the UPDI NVM controller. Segments
are classified by their virtual address against the unified UPDI
address windows: FLASH
.RB ( 0x800000 \(en 0x80FFFF ),
USERROW
.RB ( 0x810080 \(en 0x8100FF ),
EEPROM
.RB ( 0x814000 \(en 0x8143FF ),
FUSES
.RB ( 0x820000 \(en 0x82001F ),
and LOCK
.RB ( 0x820040 \(en 0x820043 ).
SIGROW segments are skipped (factory-programmed, read-only).
Segments outside every programmable window cause
.B \-\-load
to fail with a diagnostic. On any write failure the process exits 1
without opening the GDB listener.
.TP
.B \-\-erase
Issue a UPDI chip-erase before any
.B \-\-load
step. Required when the ELF contains a LOCK segment, because the
silicon requires a chip-erase to clear
.B LOCKSTATUS
before lockbits can be re-programmed.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-lock\-updi
Allow
.B \-\-load
to write LOCK byte patterns that would assert
.B UPDIDIS
and permanently disable the UPDI debug interface. Without this flag
only the 4-byte unlock pattern
.B 0x5CC5C55C
is accepted as a LOCK payload, protecting against accidental UPDI
lock-out.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-erase
Permit the GDB-side
.B monitor erase
and
.B monitor chip-erase
verbs to issue a UPDI chip-erase on the running target. Without this
flag both verbs return
.B E11
and leave the silicon untouched. Independent of the boot-time
.B \-\-erase
flag.
.TP
.B \-\-device
One-shot diagnostic. Open UPDI, run an auto-baud link-quality probe
(suppressed by
.BR \-\-no\-autobaud ),
read the SIGROW signature, revision, serial number, ASI status
registers, FUSES, and LOCK byte, print a human-readable report \(em
including the per-rung baud table and a decoded fuse listing \(em to
standard output and exit. No TCP listener is opened, no ELF is
loaded, and the target CPU is not halted. Useful as a first-line check
for wiring, target power, link quality, and the UPDIDIS fuse. Mutually
exclusive with
.BR \-\-load " and " \-\-prog .
The
.I elf-file
operand is optional in this mode.
.TP
.B \-\-prog
Program-and-exit (CI / build-server) mode. Equivalent to
.B \-\-load
followed by an immediate orderly exit \(em no GDB TCP listener is
opened, the target is
.B not
halted, and the CPU starts running the freshly programmed image. On
success prints
.B verify: OK
to standard output and exits 0. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-load " and " \-\-device .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-verify
Suppress the read-back verify pass that normally follows
.B \-\-load
or
.BR \-\-prog .
Use sparingly \(em verify is the only mechanism that detects partial
or silently-corrupted writes.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-autobaud
Used with
.B \-\-device
only. Suppress the link-quality auto-baud probe and use the rate
supplied via
.B \-\-baud
(or its default) unchanged.
.TP
.BI \-\-force\-device " family"
Override automatic AVR family detection. Accepts one of
.BR AVR-DA ,
.BR AVR-DB ,
.BR AVR-DD ,
.BR AVR-DU ,
.B AVR-SD
(case-insensitive). When set, the named family memory map is selected
unconditionally and the ELF-vs-silicon family mismatch check is
suppressed. Use this when knowingly debugging an ELF against a
different silicon family.
.SH DEVICE FAMILY SELECTION
When
.B \-\-force\-device
is not supplied,
.B avrOSdb
selects the AVR-Dx family memory map (USERROW, EEPROM, FUSES, LOCK,
SIGROW window bases and sizes) by reading SIGROW DEVICEID0..2 from
the target and matching against the AVR-DA/DB signature table.
.PP
The lowercase part-name string embedded by avr-gcc + Microchip device
packs in the
.B .note.gnu.avr.deviceinfo
ELF note (e.g.
.IR avr128da28 ,
.IR avr64dd32 ,
.IR avr32sd20 )
is
.B not
used as a selection input \(em doing so would defeat the mismatch
check below by comparing the ELF family against the family
.B avrOSdb
just told the UPDI layer to be. Instead the ELF-derived family is
used purely as an assertion against the autodetected silicon.
.PP
After selection, when the ELF carried a recognised part-name and
.B \-\-force\-device
was not used,
.B avrOSdb
verifies that the ELF-derived family matches the autodetected silicon
family. On mismatch it prints
.PP
.RS
.nf
error: ELF was built for <partname> (family <X>) but
target reports <Y> (use --force-device=<Y> to override)
.fi
.RE
.PP
to standard error and exits 1 without opening the GDB listener or
programming any NVM. Re-run with
.B \-\-force\-device=<Y>
to proceed anyway.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I serial-device
Path to the USB-serial adapter
.RI ( /dev/ttyUSB0 ", " /dev/cu.usbserial-* ", \(or similar)").
.TP
.I elf-file
AVR ELF binary used for symbol lookup and avrOS table discovery.
With
.B \-\-load
this file is also written to FLASH.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal shutdown
.RB ( SIGINT ,
.BR SIGTERM ,
or GDB
.B k
kill packet), or successful
.BR \-\-prog " / " \-\-load " verify pass.
.TP
.B 1
Bad arguments, serial-device open failure, TCP bind failure,
.B \-\-load
flash write failure,
.B \-\-device
SIGROW read failure, or family mismatch.
.TP
.B 2
Read-back verify mismatch following
.B \-\-load
or
.BR \-\-prog .
The faulting page(s) are reported on standard error with the expected
and actual CRC32 checksums.
.SH EXAMPLES
Attach to a running target without flashing:
.PP
.RS
.EX
avrOSdb /dev/ttyUSB0 firmware.elf
.EE
.RE
.PP
Flash and debug from cold on a non-default port:
.PP
.RS
.EX
avrOSdb \-\-load \-\-port 1234 \-\-baud 115200 \\
             /dev/ttyUSB0 firmware.elf
.EE
.RE
.PP
Once the server is listening, in another terminal:
.PP
.RS
.EX
avr-gdb firmware.elf
(gdb) target remote :1234
(gdb) info threads
(gdb) monitor avros events
.EE
.RE
.PP
Verify the UPDI link without starting a GDB listener:
.PP
.RS
.EX
avrOSdb \-\-device /dev/ttyUSB0
.EE
.RE
.PP
Allow GDB to chip-erase the running target via
.BR "monitor erase" :
.PP
.RS
.EX
avrOSdb \-\-allow\-erase /dev/ttyUSB0 firmware.elf
.EE
.RE
.PP
From GDB the Phase-10 surface offers
.BR "load" " (vFlashErase/Write/Done),"
.BR "watch" / "rwatch" / "awatch" " (Z2/Z3/Z4 data watchpoints),"
.BR "run" / "kill" " (vRun/vKill), and software breakpoints via"
.BR Z0 " (FLASH"
.B BREAK
opcode by default, switchable via
.BR "monitor bp-mode" ).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR avr-gdb (1),
.BR avrdude (1),
.BR termios (3)
